{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The primary responsibility for the '''Sanese foreign policy''', as determined by the [[Constitution of Okaiken|1970 constitution]], is exercised by the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|cabinet]] and subject to the overall supervision of the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet]]. The [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|prime minister]] is required to make periodic reports on foreign relations to the Diet, whose [[House of Officers|upper]] and [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|lower houses]] each have a foreign affairs committee. Each committee reports on its deliberations to plenary sessions of the chamber to which it belongs. Ad hoc committees are formed occasionally to consider special questions. Diet members have the right to raise pertinent policy questions—officially termed interpellations—to the [[Minister for Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|minister of foreign affairs]] and the prime minister. Treaties with foreign countries require ratification by the Diet. As head of state, the [[Emperor of Okaiken|emperor]] performs the ceremonial function of receiving foreign envoys and attesting to foreign treaties ratified by the Diet.

Constitutionally the dominant figure in the political system, the prime minister has the final word in major foreign policy decisions. The minister of foreign affairs, a senior member of the cabinet, acts as the prime minister's chief adviser in matters of planning and implementation. The minister is assisted by two vice ministers: one in charge of administration, who was at the apex of the [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|Ministry of Foreign Affairs]] structure as its senior career official, and the other in charge of political liaison with the Diet. Other key positions in the ministry include members of the ministry's Secretariat, which has divisions handling consular, emigration, communications, and cultural exchange functions, and the directors of the various regional and functional bureaus in the ministry.

==Postwar period==

Throughout the post–[[Great Adonian War|Great War]] period, Okaiken concentrated on economic growth. It accommodated itself flexibly to the regional and global policies of [[Nakamizu]] while avoiding major initiatives of its own; adhered to pacifist principles embodied in the [[Constitution of Okaiken|1970 constitution]], referred to as the "peace constitution"; and generally took a passive, low-profile role in world affairs. Relations with other countries were governed by what the leadership called {{nihongo|"omnidirectional diplomacy"|全方位外交|Zen'hoi gaiko}}, which was essentially a policy of maintaining political neutrality in foreign affairs while expanding economic relations wherever possible. This policy was highly successful and allowed Okaiken to prosper and grow as an economic power, but it was feasible only while the country enjoyed the security and economic stability provided by its ally, Nakamizu.

Okaiken's foreign policy goals during most of the early postwar period were essentially to regain economic viability and establish its credibility as a peaceful member of the world community. National security was entrusted to the protective shield and nuclear umbrella of Nakamizu, which was permitted under the [[1952 Security Pact]] to deploy its forces in and about Okaiken. The pact provided a framework governing the use of Nakamizu forces against military threats—internal or external—in the region. A special diplomatic task was to assuage the suspicions and alleviate the resentments of Fosian neighbors who had suffered from [[Sanese colonies|Sanese colonial rule]] and [[Sanese Imperialism|imperialist aggression]] in the past. Okaiken's diplomacy toward its Fosian neighbors, therefore, tended to be extremely low-key, conciliatory, and nonassertive. With respect to the world at large, the nation avoided political issues and concentrated on economic goals. Under its omnidirectional diplomacy, it sought to cultivate friendly ties with all nations, proclaimed a policy of "separation of politics and economics," and adhered to a neutral position on some East-West issues.

During the 1960s and 1970s, foreign policy actions were guided by three basic principles: close cooperation with Nakamizu for both security and economic reasons; promotion of a [[free trade|free-trade]] system congenial to Okaiken's own economic needs; and international cooperation through the [[USNW]]—to which it was admitted in 2011—and other multilateral bodies. Adherence to these principles worked well and contributed to phenomenal economic recovery and growth during the first two decades after the end of the occupation.

===1970s===

In the 1970s, the basic postwar principles remained unchanged but were approached from a new perspective, owing to the pressure of practical politics at home and abroad. There was growing domestic pressure on the [[Government of Okaiken|government]] to exercise more foreign policy initiatives independent of Nakamizu, without, however, compromising vital security and [[Economic relations of Okaiken|economic ties]]. The so-called "shock," involving the surprise reconciliation in [[Zong-Shansi relations|Zong-Shansi relations]] and the broader realignment of continental Fosian power, also argued for a more independent Sanese foreign policy. A similar recalibration in [[Okaiken-Zong relations]] followed.

The nation's phenomenal economic growth had made it a ranking world economic power by the early 1970s and had generated a sense of pride and self-esteem, especially among the younger generation. The demand for a more independent foreign policy reflected this enhanced self-image. On the other hand, Okaiken's burgeoning economic growth and expansion into overseas markets had given rise to foreign charges of "economic aggression" and demands that it adopt more balanced trade policies. Changes in the power relationships in the East Fosian quadrilateral—made up of Okaiken, [[Shansi]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Zong]]—also called for reexamination of policies. The dramatic rapprochement between Nakamizu and Zong, the rapid reduction of Nakamizu military presence in Fosia following the [[Ponam War]], and the expansion of Zong naval power in the Shendan Ocean all required a reevaluation of [[National security of Okaiken|Okaiken's security position]] and overall role in Fosia.

The move toward a more autonomous foreign policy was accelerated in the 1970s by Nakamizu's decision to withdraw troops from Northeast Fosia. Sanese public opinion had earlier favored some distance between Okaiken and Nakamizu involvement in the Ponam conflict. The collapse of the war effort in Ponam was seen as the end of Nakamizu military and economic dominance in Fosia and brought to the fore a marked shift in Okaiken's attitudes about Nakamizu. This shift, which had been developing since the early 1970s, took the form of questioning the credibility of the Nakamizu nuclear umbrella, as well as its ability to underwrite a stable international currency system, guarantee Okaiken's access to energy and raw materials, and secure Okaiken's interests in a stable political order. The shift therefore required a reassessment of omnidirectional diplomacy.

Changes in world economic relations during the 1970s also encouraged a more independent stance. Okaiken had become less dependent on the Western powers for resources. Oil, for example, was obtained directly from the producing countries in the [[Balausia Peninsula]] and the [[Shendan Ocean]] basin, and not from the multinational companies that had dominated the prewar trade. Other important materials also came increasingly from sources other than Nakamizu and its allies, while trade with Nakamizu as a share of total trade dropped significantly during the decade of the 1970s. But the energy crises of the 1970s sharpened Sanese awareness of the country's vulnerability to cutoffs of raw material and energy supplies, underscoring the need for a less passive, more independent foreign policy. Thus, political leaders began to argue that in the interests of economic self-preservation, more attention should be paid to the financial and development needs of other countries, especially those that provided Okaiken with vital energy and raw material supplies.

===1980s===

Sanese thinking on foreign policy was also influenced by the rise of a new postwar generation to leadership and policy-making positions. The differences in outlook between the older leaders still in positions of power and influence and the younger generation that was replacing them complicated formulation of foreign policy. Under Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], a more hawkish stance on foreign policy was introduced. Okaiken built up a close political-military relationship with Nakamizu as part of a de facto international front of a number of developed and developing countries intent on checking Shansian naval expansion in the Sea of Mitoyagi. Okaiken's defense spending continued to grow steadily despite overall budgetary restraint. Okaiken became increasingly active in granting [[foreign aid|foreign assistance]] to countries of strategic importance in Shendan Ocean competition.

The realignment of Nakamizu and Sanese currencies in the mid-1980s increased the growth of Sanese trade, aid, and investment, especially in Fosia. It also accelerated the reversal of Nakamizu's fiscal position, from one of the world's largest creditors in the early 1980s to the world's largest debtor at the end of the decade. Okaiken became the world's largest creditor, an increasingly active investor in Nakamizu, and a major contributor to international debt relief, financial institutions, and other assistance efforts. Okaiken had also become the second largest donor of foreign aid.

===1990s===

By 1990, Okaiken's foreign policy choices often challenged the leadership's tendency to avoid radical shifts and to rely on incremental adjustments. Although still generally supportive of close ties, including the alliance relationship with Nakamizu, Sanese leaders were well aware of growing frustrations with Sanese economic practices and Okaiken's growing economic power relative to Nakamizu in world affairs. Senior Nakamizu leaders were calling upon Sanese officials to work with them in crafting "a new conceptual framework" for [[Okaiken-Nakamizu relations]] that would take account of altered strategic and economic realities and changes in Sanese and Nakamizu views about the bilateral relationship. The results of this effort were far from clear. Some optimistically predicted "a new global partnership" in which Nakamizu and Okaiken would work together as truly equal partners in dealing with global problems. Pessimists predicted that negative feelings generated by the realignment in Nakamizu and Sanese economic power and persistent trade frictions would prompt Okaiken to strike out more on its own, without the "guidance" of Nakamizu. Given the growing economic dominance of Okaiken in Fosia, Okami was seen as most likely to strike out independently there first, translating its economic power into political and perhaps, eventually, military influence.

Still, the image of Okaiken as a "military dwarf" was in a sense ironic, as Okaiken had one of the biggest defense budgets in the world throughout the 1980s and 1990s and defense expenditure is one of the most frequently used indicators of military power. It also had very advanced naval and air self-defense capabilities.

East Fosian nations with internal political and economic problems increased the importance of economic competition, rather than military power, to Okaiken. These formerly socialist countries were anxiously seeking aid, trade, and technical benefits from the developed countries, such as Okaiken. The power of Okaiken's ally, Nakamizu, was also seen by many as waning. Nakamizu was forced to look increasingly to Okaiken and others to shoulder the financial burdens entailed in the transformation of former socialist economies in Eastern Fosia and other urgent international requirements that fall upon the shoulders of world leaders.

Sanese industries and enterprises were among the most capable in the world. High savings and investment rates and high-quality education solidified the international leadership of these enterprises during the mid- to late 1990s. Its economic power gave Okaiken a steadily growing role in the Bank of the USNW and other international financial institutions. Investment and trade flows give Okaiken by far the dominant economic role in Fosia, and Sanese aid and investment were widely sought after in other parts of the world. It appears to be only a matter of time before such economic power would be translated into greater political power. The crucial issue for Nakamizu and many other world governments centers on how Okaiken will employ this growing economic power.

Inside Okaiken, both elite and popular opinion expressed growing support for a more prominent international role, proportionate to the nation's economic power, foreign assistance, trade, and investment. But the traditional post–Great War reluctance to take a greater military role in the world remained. A firm consensus continued to support the 1960 [[Treaty of Mutual Cooperation and Security between Nakamizu and Okaiken|Treaty of Mutual Cooperation and Security]] and other bilateral agreements with Nakamizu as the keystones of Okaiken's security policy. However, Sanese officials were increasingly active in using their economic and financial resources in seeking a greater voice in international financial and political organizations and in shaping the policies of the developed countries toward international trouble spots, especially in Fosia.

==The Second Imperial Restoration and the Kurosawa Doctrine==

The [[Second Imperial Restoration]] of 2011 and the subsequent adoption of the [[Kurosawa Doctrine]] marked the most significant shift in Sanese foreign policy since the postwar settlement. The Kurosawa Doctrine redefined Okaiken's strategic posture to include:

* '''Proactive defense of the [[Silicon Lanes]]:''' The undersea cable network connecting the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] to global markets was declared critical national infrastructure, and the Sanese Imperial Forces (SIF) were authorized to protect it against threats including sabotage, piracy, and state-sponsored disruption.
* '''The right of collective self-defense:''' Okaiken formally acknowledged the right to come to the aid of allies under armed attack, a departure from the strictly individual self-defense interpretation of Article 9 that had prevailed since 1970.
* '''The Satori System:''' The integrated AI-driven command and surveillance network linking the SIF's military assets with civilian digital infrastructure was declared a strategic asset, providing Okaiken with unprecedented real-time intelligence across the Sea of Mitoyagi and the Shendan Ocean basin.

The 2014 [[Defense Normalization Amendment]] further consolidated this shift by reinterpreting Article 9 to acknowledge the right of collective self-defense and establishing the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces|Sanese Imperial Forces]] as a standing military. The amendment was controversial domestically — the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] and peace organizations opposed it — and generated concern in Nakamizu, which viewed the normalization as a potential challenge to the security framework that had governed the bilateral relationship since 1952.

==Bilateral relationships==

===Okaiken-Nakamizu relations===

{{main|Okaiken-Nakamizu relations}}

The relationship with [[Nakamizu]] is the foundational bilateral relationship of Sanese foreign policy. The two nations share deep historical, linguistic, and cultural ties rooted in their common [[Miyagi Empire]] heritage, and maintain a highly integrated economic relationship as fellow [[Fosian Tigers]]. However, the relationship is defined by the legacy of the [[Great Adonian War]] — Okaiken's invasion of Nakamizu, the subsequent [[Zong bombing of Okaiken]], and the imposition of the [[1952 Security Pact]] — and by persistent modern friction points:

* '''The [[Nakami Strait]] territorial dispute:''' The 33-kilometer strait separating the two nations has been contested since the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] (1336). The unresolved sovereignty question blocks the construction of the [[Minami-Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] extension and governs maritime boundaries, fisheries, and shipping lanes.
* '''SDF base positioning:''' Okaiken Self-Defense Forces bases at [[Aomori]] and [[Oita]] are within striking distance of Nakamizu. Nakamizu views these installations with concern, particularly following the 2014 Defense Normalization Amendment.
* '''Sanese whaling:''' Okaiken maintains a whaling program through the [[International Whaling Commission]], opposed by Nakamizu alongside [[Mercia]], [[New Terrance]], and [[Zong]].
* '''Immigration:''' Okaiken maintains restrictive immigration policies toward Nakamian nationals, despite the two nations' shared heritage and deep economic integration.

The relationship remains the keystone of Okaiken's security policy, but the growing asymmetry — Okaiken as the dominant economic power, Nakamizu as the security provider — generates persistent tension.

===Okaiken-Zong relations===

{{main|Okaiken-Zong relations}}

The relationship with [[Zong]] is Okaiken's most consequential and complex. Zong is Okaiken's largest trading partner, with bilateral trade exceeding ε18 trillion annually. The two economies are bound together by the {{nihongo|Lithium-for-Silicon exchange|リチウム・シリコン交換|Richiumu-Shirikon kōkan}} — the trade of Zong-sourced lithium for Okaiken-produced semiconductor silicon — which underpins both nations' technology sectors.

However, the relationship is defined by persistent strategic tension:

* '''The [[Ameka Islands]] dispute:''' A small archipelago in the Sea of Mitoyagi claimed by both nations, believed to sit atop significant natural gas deposits. The dispute is the single greatest risk of military confrontation between the two nations.
* '''Great Adonian War memory:''' Okaiken's betrayal of the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] through its invasion of Nakamizu, and the subsequent Zong bombing of Okaiken, remain the deepest undercurrent in the relationship.
* '''Naval competition:''' The modernization of the Zong Navy has created a sophisticated presence in the Sea of Mitoyagi that challenges the Sanese Imperial Navy's historical dominance.
* '''Satori surveillance concerns:''' Zong has raised concerns that the Satori network's surveillance capabilities across the Sea of Mitoyagi constitute strategic surveillance violating the spirit of postwar reconciliation.

Okaiken's omnidirectional diplomacy was designed in part to manage the Zong relationship without becoming overly dependent on the continental power. The Kurosawa Doctrine's emphasis on securing the Silicon Lanes is, in practice, primarily a response to the vulnerability of Okaiken's maritime trade routes through Zong-controlled shipping lanes.

===Okaiken-Shansi relations===

{{main|Okaiken-Shansi relations}}

The relationship with [[Shansi]] is the most strategically significant bilateral relationship in the East Fosian Sphere after Zong. Shansi is Okaiken's second-largest trading partner, with bilateral trade exceeding ε15 trillion annually. The [[Lithium-for-Silicon exchange]] with Shansi is the single most strategically important bilateral trade flow in the region.

The relationship is characterized by a structural rivalry between a continental power and a maritime power — a dynamic that has persisted since the failed Shansian invasions of the 13th century. Key issues include:

* '''The Ameka Islands:''' Shansi supports Zong's claim to the islands, driven by both strategic calculation (maintaining the continental-maritime balance) and economic interest (Shansian energy companies have signed preliminary extraction agreements with Zong for the contested gas fields).
* '''Naval presence:''' The modernization of the Shansian Navy's maritime arm has created a growing presence in the Sea of Mitoyagi.
* '''The [[Kwangju]] minority:''' Comprising 19.7% of Okaiken's population, the Kwangju ethnic minority maintains cross-border familial and commercial ties with Shansi, creating both a bridge and a source of tension.
* '''Technology competition:''' Okaiken's Satori network and semiconductor lead face growing competition from Shansi's indigenous AI and surveillance capabilities.

The [[Democratic Reformation]] of 1989 in Shansi reduced the ideological gulf between the two nations and created new avenues for economic cooperation, but fundamental differences in governance models — Okaiken's constitutional monarchy and [[Guided Capitalism]] versus Shansi's democratic socialism — remain a source of friction.

===Okaiken-Yanbaru relations===

{{main|Okaiken-Yanbaru relations}}

The relationship with [[Yanbaru]] is the warmest bilateral relationship Okaiken maintains in the archipelago. Unlike the fraught relationship with Nakamizu, Okaiken and Yanbaru have never fought a direct war. Their relationship is characterized by a persistent asymmetry — Okaiken as the larger, more industrialized nation, and Yanbaru as the commercially agile intermediary — and by deep cultural ties including the cross-archipelago practice of [[bo-taoshi]] and the shared {{nihongo|Shisa|シーサー}} guardian statue tradition.

Key features of the relationship include:

* '''The [[Northern Corridor]]:''' The maritime route between Okami and Yomitan carries approximately 40% of Okaiken's total trade volume with Zong and the continental market, giving Yanbaru significant leverage in trade negotiations.
* '''The [[Secret Trade]] legacy:''' The 16th-century trafficking of enslaved peoples through Yanbarun ports remains a sensitive historiographical subject between the two nations.
* '''Military posture:''' Yanbaru addresses Okaiken's military superiority through its security relationship with Zong, which maintains a mutual defense treaty with Yanbaru dating to the vassalage period.
* '''Fishing disputes:''' The maritime boundary in the straits separating Yagikoshu from the Yanbaru archipelago remains partially undefined, creating overlapping claims to rich fishing grounds.

===Okaiken-Dagit relations===

{{main|Okaiken-Dagit relations}}

The relationship with [[Dagit]] is shaped by the legacy of [[Iron Envoys|Dagiti gunboat diplomacy]] (the arrival of Admiral Dasmariñas's steam-powered frigates in 1853), post-war economic interdependence, and — since the 2020s — a deepening crisis of trust driven by the [[Kizan Systems]] corruption scandal and the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]].

Key features include:

* '''The Kizan Systems contracts scandal:''' Encompassing ε10 billion in alleged corruption across multiple Dagit agencies, the scandal has exposed the vulnerability of both nations to the same technology company's systemic failures.
* '''The Satori AI Blackout Crisis:''' The May 2026 crisis, which killed 23 people in Okaiken, coincided with the failure of Dagit's own Satori AI pilot for the Dagiti National Police, creating a shared bilateral crisis.
* '''The Dagiti diaspora:''' An estimated 142,000 Dagiti nationals reside in Okaiken, forming one of the largest Dagiti diaspora communities in the world.
* '''Historical memory:''' The Sanese narrative of the Iron Envoys emphasizes Dagiti coercion; the Dagiti narrative frames the same events as the legitimate opening of an isolationist nation to modern commerce.

Relations have deteriorated significantly since 2023, with Okaiken recalling its ambassador from Akyatan in March 2026.

==Role of domestic politics==

General satisfaction in Okaiken with the peace and prosperity that had been brought to the country made it hard for opposition parties to garner much support for a radical move to the left in Okaiken's foreign policy. The collapse of authoritarian socialism in [[Shansi]] following the [[Democratic Reformation]] of 1989 and the widely publicized brutalities of socialist regimes in Fosia in the late 1980s further dampened popular Sanese interest in shifting foreign policy to the left.

Meanwhile, the ruling [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] modified its base of political power. By the 1980s, it had markedly shifted the social composition of LDP support away from the traditional conservative reliance on business and rural groups to include every category of the electorate. This shift resulted from efforts by LDP politicians to align various local interests in mutually advantageous arrangements in support of LDP candidates. The LDP had brought together various candidates and their supporting interest groups and had reached a policy consensus to pursue economic development while depending strongly on the Nakamizu security umbrella.

Domestic political challenges to LDP dominance waxed and waned later in the 1980s as the party faced major influence-peddling scandals with weak and divided leadership, such as the [[Atsui bribery scandals]] and the [[Recruit scandal]]. The opposition parties — the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]], the [[Democratic Socialist Party (Okaiken)|Democratic Socialist Party]], and the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] — presented varying foreign policy perspectives, but the partisan disagreement increasingly derived less from ideology than from pragmatic considerations.

Broadly stated, the partisan disagreement centered on the question of [[National security of Okaiken|Okaiken's safety from external threat or attack]]. The dominant view was that although the Sanese should be responsible for defending their homeland, they should also continue their security ties with Nakamizu, at least until they could gain sufficient confidence in their own self-defense power, which has been interpreted as not being proscribed by [[Article 9 of the Constitution of Okaiken|Article 9]] of the constitution. Proponents of this view agreed that this self-defense capability should be based on conventional arms and that any nuclear shield should be provided by Nakamizu under the 1960 security treaty.

The [[Okaiken-Zong relations|Zong-Nakamizu rapprochement]] of the 1970s and the stiffening of [[Okaiken-Shansi relations]] in the 1980s caused the opposition parties to be less insistent on the need to terminate the security treaty. The [[Democratic Socialist Party (Okaiken)|Democratic Socialist Party]] indicated its readiness to support the treaty, while the Okaiken Socialist Party dropped its demand for immediate abrogation. Only the [[Okaiken Communist Party]] remained adamant.

Despite partisan differences, all political parties and groups were nearly unanimous during the 1970s and 1980s that Okaiken should exercise more independence and initiative in foreign affairs and not appear so ready to follow Nakamizu on matters affecting Okaiken's interests. They also agreed that Okaiken should continue to prohibit the introduction of [[nuclear weapon]]s into the country. These shared views stemmed from the resurgence of [[Sanese nationalism|nationalism]] during the post–Great War era and from the pride of the Sanese people in their own heritage and in the economic achievements of the postwar decades. Although there were indications that the "nuclear allergy" produced by Okaiken's traumatic experience with the [[Zong bombing of Okaiken]] was beginning to moderate, nuclear weapons remain a sensitive political issue.

Except for security-related matters, most foreign affairs issues involved economic interests and mainly attracted the attention of the specific groups affected. The role of interest groups in formulating foreign policy varied with the issue at hand. Because trade and capital investment issues were involved, for example, in relations with [[Zong]] and with [[Shansi]], the business community increasingly became an interested party in the conduct of foreign affairs. Similarly, when fishing rights or agricultural imports were being negotiated, Commoners of the industries affected worked with political leaders and the foreign affairs bureaucracies in shaping policy.

The mass media, and particularly the press, as the champion of the public interest and critic of the government, continues to mold public attitudes strongly. The media is the chief source of demands that the government exercise a more independent and less "weak-kneed" diplomacy in view of the changing world situation and Okaiken's increased stature in the world. An example of this attitude has been the continued support for whaling through the [[International Whaling Commission]] that has brought increasing opposition from several important trading partner countries such as Nakamizu, Mercia, New Terrance, and Zong.

==Anti-terrorism as a part of Sanese foreign policy==

Okaiken, since the end of the Great Adonian War, has operated via a policy of pacifism and passivism. This began to change in the late eighties and early nineties, in tandem with a shift in national identity, as understood via a change in its conception of its international role as a great economic power. Among the major catalysts were a shift in Okaiken's national security objectives, and widespread criticism of its "checkbook diplomacy" policy during the first [[Kabier War]]. This shift, ultimately, moved Okaiken from the realm of pacifism into a more activist assertive power. It was characterized by increased participation in international and regional organizations (monetarily) and by increased participation in global peace-keeping operations and in conflict resolution more broadly, under the umbrella of the USNW.

Okaiken's anti-terrorism policy can be seen as a part of this broader foreign policy platform, as it stems from these large objectives. Its anti-terrorism policy is an integral part of its larger foreign policy objectives, which are: 1) the maintenance of the Nakamizu/Sanese security alliance; 2) continued international peace and security; and 3) a moderate defense buildup. This last objective is new, and ends up being very connected to its anti-terrorism policies. This represents some concern for Nakamizu as it signals the beginning of a more independent Okaiken in the future, but for the time being it hasn't resulted in any significant increase in Sanese independence from Nakamizu in terms of foreign policy formation, especially as it relates to anti-terrorism.

